      q: "What is the difference between component testing and unit testing?",
      a: "Unit tests verify that pure functions return the correct output for a given input — they run in milliseconds and have no DOM dependency. Component tests render a UI component (or a small tree) and assert the rendered output, user interactions, and accessibility — they run in seconds and require a DOM environment (jsdom or a real browser). The key distinction: a unit test for a Button component might assert that <code>formatLabel('submit')</code> returns 'Submit' — but it says nothing about whether the button renders, whether it is clickable, or whether a screen reader can identify it. A component test mounts the Button, asserts that the rendered text is 'Submit', that clicking it calls the onClick handler, and that it has <code>role='button'</code> — giving confidence that the component works as a user would experience it. The interview answer that signals seniority: 'Unit tests verify logic. Component tests verify behaviour. Both are necessary, but they test different things. The common mistake is writing component tests that assert implementation details — component state, internal function calls — which makes them unit tests that happen to render. Component tests should assert user-visible output: text content, ARIA attributes, visual states, and the effects of user interactions.'"
