      q: "Should I use Playwright Component Testing or Cypress Component Testing in 2026?",
      a: "It depends on your existing stack and your testing priorities. If you already use Playwright for end-to-end tests, Playwright CT is the natural choice — it shares the same test runner, the same locator API, the same trace viewer, and the same CI configuration. You get visual comparison (<code>toHaveScreenshot()</code>), network interception (<code>page.route()</code>), and real-browser rendering — all in the same framework you already use for e2e tests. If you already use Cypress for end-to-end tests, Cypress CT is the natural choice for the same reasons — shared API, shared CI, shared test runner. If you are starting from scratch in 2026, Playwright CT has the edge for three reasons: (1) multi-browser support — Playwright CT runs components in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit, while Cypress CT is Chromium-based (with experimental Firefox/WebKit support); (2) visual comparison is first-class in Playwright, while Cypress requires a plugin; (3) Playwright's auto-waiting model is more resilient to async rendering than Cypress's retry-ability model for component tests. However, if your team values Cypress's Time Travel debugging (stepping through each command in the Cypress runner) and its plugin ecosystem, Cypress CT remains a strong choice. For fast, jsdom-based component tests without a real browser, Testing Library is the standard and works with both Playwright and Cypress e2e suites. The senior-level answer: 'I use Testing Library for fast pre-commit component tests (under 2 seconds per file), Playwright CT for visual and cross-browser component validation in CI (under 5 minutes for the full suite), and Playwright e2e for critical user journeys (under 10 minutes for the full suite). Three layers, each optimised for what they do best.'"
