      q: "How do I test components that fetch data from APIs?",
      a: "Mock the API at the network level — not the function level. Use MSW (Mock Service Worker) or Playwright's <code>page.route()</code> / Cypress's <code>cy.intercept()</code> to intercept the HTTP request and return your test data. Test three states: loading (assert the loading spinner is visible and has <code>role='status'</code>), success (assert the data is rendered correctly — text content, list items, table rows), and error (assert the error message is visible and has <code>role='alert'</code>). The common anti-pattern: mocking the data-fetching function (<code>jest.mock('./api', () => ({ fetchUsers: jest.fn() }))</code>) instead of intercepting the network request. Function-level mocks do not test that the component makes the correct fetch call — correct URL, correct method, correct headers. Network-level mocks do. The mastery pattern: use MSW handlers that return realistic data, including edge cases — empty arrays, null fields, long strings, special characters — and test that the component handles each case gracefully. At the Co-op, a component test with MSW caught a bug where the offers-carousel API returned an empty array after a deployment change, and the component rendered a blank white section instead of the empty-state message — a bug that function-level mocks never caught because the mock always returned a populated array."
