      q: "What is the difference between mount() and render() in component testing?",
      a: "<code>mount()</code> is the Playwright CT and Cypress CT API for rendering a component in a real browser. It mounts the component as if it were part of a real page — with real CSS, real event handling, and real layout. <code>render()</code> is the Testing Library API for rendering a component in jsdom — a simulated DOM environment in Node. The practical differences: <code>mount()</code> renders in a real browser (Chromium/Firefox/WebKit), supports visual comparison and cross-browser testing, and can test CSS layout, media queries, and hover/focus styles. <code>render()</code> runs in jsdom (Node), is faster (no browser startup), and is simpler to set up (no browser binary download). The interview answer: '<code>mount()</code> when I need visual fidelity or cross-browser testing. <code>render()</code> when I need speed and simplicity. Most component tests — form validation, list rendering, event handling — work correctly in jsdom and benefit from the speed. Components with complex CSS, responsive layouts, or browser-specific behaviour need <code>mount()</code>.'"
